-- title:
--   Theorem 2.2.3 — history-dependent policies do not improve the value (corrected: $E$, $A$ Borel spaces)
-- statement:
--   Let $E$ and $A$ be Borel spaces and let $(A_N)$ hold. For $n=0,\dots,N$ and every history $h_n=(x_0,a_0,x_1,\dots,x_n)\in H_n$,
--
--   $$V_n(x_n)=\sup_{\pi\in\Pi_N}V_{n\pi}(h_n),$$
--
--   where $\Pi_N$ is the set of history-dependent $N$-stage policies and $V_n$ is the value function over Markov policies.
--
--   So the controller gains nothing by remembering the past: the value depends on the history through the current state only. This justifies restricting attention to Markov policies.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** Borel spaces are `StandardBorelSpace`, the book's notion (Remark 2.1.2 a)). $(A_N)$ is the book's standing assumption.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - correction: the statement is made for the general measurable spaces of Definition 2.1.1 → E and A are standard Borel spaces; reason: on general spaces it is false. Let h be a function on [0,1] whose graph S has full outer measure in the unit square, P the outer measure on the trace σ-algebra 𝔅 of the Borel sets on S, and 𝒜 ⊂ 𝔅 the σ-algebra generated by the first coordinate. Let E be the disjoint union of a point o, a copy S₁ of (S, 𝔅) and a copy S₂ of (S, 𝒜), and A = (S, 𝔅), D_n = E × A, N = 3. From o the next state has law P on S₁, a state s of S₁ moves to s in S₂, S₂ is absorbing, and the only reward is r_2(y, a) = 1 if y ∈ S₂ and a = y, which is measurable because the first coordinate is injective on S. The history-dependent rule a_2 = x_1 earns 1. A Markov rule f : S₂ → A earns P(f = id) ≤ 1/2, since for C = S ∩ ([0,1] × [0,1/2]) the set f⁻¹(C) is of the form S ∩ (B × [0,1]) and differs from C on a set of P-measure 1/2. So V_0(o) ≤ 1/2 < 1; on Borel spaces the measurable selection of the proof exists
--   - reading: V_{nπ}(h_n) is the conditional expectation of the total reward in [n, N], given the history h_n → the expectation under the continuation pathKernel of h_n under π, counting the rewards of times n, …, N−1 and the terminal reward; reason: the book gives no formula; this is the regular version of the conditional expectation
--   - encoding: Q_n and r_n are defined on D_n → Q_n and r_n are defined on E × A (MDPFinance.Bellman.MarkovDecisionModel); reason: D_n is measurable, so every book model extends (for example by δ_x and 0), and values off D_n enter no statement
--   - encoding: E and A are sets → E and A lie in one universe, as in MDPFinance.Bellman.Hist; reason: a Lean universe convention; no mathematical instance is lost, since a space can be lifted
--   - encoding: 𝔼^π_{nx} in V_n is the expectation under the conditional probability ℙ^π(· | X_n = x) → the expectation under the kernel pathKernel, forward composition of the Q_k, recording states and actions; reason: this is the regular version of the conditional law, defined at every x
--   - encoding: expectations of the real total reward → erealIntegral, the EReal difference of the Lebesgue integrals of the positive and negative parts, with −∞ when both are infinite; reason: on Borel spaces (A_N) makes the positive part finite under Markov and history-dependent policies alike, so that case does not occur
-- source:
--   Bäuerle & Rieder, Markov Decision Processes with Applications to Finance (2011), Theorem 2.2.3, p.19

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_MDPFinance_Bellman_Model
import Definitions.Def_MDPFinance_Bellman_Policy
import Definitions.Def_MDPFinance_Bellman_Operators
import Definitions.Def_MDPFinance_Bellman_History
import Definitions.Def_BauerleRiederMDP_FiniteHorizon_Policy
import Definitions.Def_BauerleRiederMDP_FiniteHorizon_stepKernel
import Definitions.Def_BauerleRiederMDP_FiniteHorizon_pathKernel
import Definitions.Def_BauerleRiederMDP_FiniteHorizon_pathReward
import Definitions.Def_BauerleRiederMDP_FiniteHorizon_markovRule
import Definitions.Def_BauerleRiederMDP_FiniteHorizon_Vpi
import Definitions.Def_BauerleRiederMDP_FiniteHorizon_V
import Definitions.Def_BauerleRiederMDP_FiniteHorizon_deltaN
import Definitions.Def_BauerleRiederMDP_FiniteHorizon_IntegrabilityAssumption
import Definitions.Def_BauerleRiederMDP_FiniteHorizon_histRule
import Definitions.Def_BauerleRiederMDP_FiniteHorizon_VHist

open MeasureTheory ProbabilityTheory
open MDPFinance.Bellman (MarkovDecisionModel Hist HistPolicy histState)

namespace BauerleRiederMDP.FiniteHorizon

universe u

/-- Theorem 2.2.3 (Bäuerle & Rieder (2011), p.19, PDF 34), for Borel state and action spaces: for
`n = 0, …, N` it holds `V_n(x_n) = sup_{π ∈ Π_N} V_{nπ}(h_n)`, `h_n = (x_0, a_0, x_1, …, x_n)`.

**Formalization Note.** The book states this on arbitrary measurable spaces, citing Hinderer
(1970), Theorem 18.4. There it fails: see the Differences list of this item for the
counterexample. `[StandardBorelSpace E] [StandardBorelSpace A]` is the book's "Borel spaces"
(Remark 2.1.2 a), p.14, and p.29). (A_N) is the book's standing assumption. -/
theorem theorem_2_2_3 {E A : Type u} [MeasurableSpace E] [MeasurableSpace A]
    [StandardBorelSpace E] [StandardBorelSpace A] {N : ℕ}
    (M : MarkovDecisionModel E A N) (hAN : IntegrabilityAssumption M)
    (n : ℕ) (hn : n ≤ N) (h : Hist E A n) :
    V M n (histState n h) = ⨆ π : HistPolicy M, VHist M π n h := by sorry

end BauerleRiederMDP.FiniteHorizon
